Fillets: a stripe rolled on past a tight tangent face at its end and cut
(patch 1530) is one face from its last walked piece's start to its end
point (mitcad#1418).

Patch 1530 ends a walk whose contact on a face F crosses a tangent edge
onto a face B at the spine's end that no ball of the radius touches: the
blend is walked on from the last piece P's end section with F's surface
unbounded, cut by B, and ends in a point on the spine's last edge. P and
the cut piece were two faces (the walked piece up to the section through
the crossing and the cut piece after it, 1.547501 and 0.052383 mm2 at each
of the four ends of the case) where the stored body has one face of
1.599656 mm2: the ball rolls on over F's surface through the crossing as
before it, one surface.

WalkedCutToEnd now walks the blend on from P's other end, through the
crossing, up to the end: P is the part of that walk up to the section
through the crossing (its contacts' lines, P's points at its other end),
and the piece cut by B is the rest, on the same surface, so that the two
are one face (ChFi3d_FilDS makes no section between pieces on one
surface). Where that walk does not pass through the crossing, the blend is
walked from P's end section as before.

Test: geometry/tests/test_fillet_tight_tangent_end.cpp: each end's face
has the stored body's area, and the result the stored body's faces.

Upstream: new.

